150:It reads no map, serves tensor parallel - on Qwen up to its four key heads, or eight ranks with each key head on two that sum their block scores - with its decode in a CUDA graph and its prefill eager, and refuses context parallel and a kernel run.
198:  model.indexer.top_k: [2048, 4096]   # -> ..._top_k2048, ..._top_k4096
279:PaperTrail has a phase of its own, because its questions share a context of up to 10M tokens: one context is prefilled at a time, by a request of one token, before its questions go.
283:A pooled worker whose task is capped at the server's own cap keeps one request more in flight, which waits in the server's queue - the server takes in a 10M prompt for about a minute, and that now happens while the request ahead decodes rather than with the GPUs idle.
284:The server still runs no more requests at once, and a shared context's first question still goes alone, so no answer changes; a waiting row's `seconds` is its time in a slot, with the wait in `queued_seconds`.
331:Every server a record names carries its `mesh`, and so does a kernel record's setup: each axis's size even at 1, whether prefill context parallel was on and how it split a chunk, the attention data parallel, the node count, the MoE runner and the image.
337:| `cp` | a prefill chunk's rows - prefill context parallel |
338:| `dcp` | the KV pool's slots - decode context parallel, on attention's ranks |
342:So eight GPUs read `tp8-cp1-dcp1-pp1-ep8` under tensor parallel and `tp1-cp8-dcp8-pp1-ep8` under context parallel, though the server reports a TP of 8 for both.
349:A launch that ended well is `complete` when the record holds everything the run set out to measure, and `partial` when it does not - a planned task with no result or fewer samples scored than planned, a kernel context that did not finish.
366:`contexts` is the ladder, one harness process each, measured in the order given so an interrupted run keeps the short points it already has.
368:A context whose samples are already on disk is kept, which is what makes a job that ran out of wall clock finishable rather than restartable - resubmitted with `run.id` pinned to its directory.
369:The record is rewritten after every context, so a run that is still climbing is readable.
