110: However, the indexer has a cost. Since it must score all preceding keys, it typically remains 𝒪 ⁡ ( T 2 ) \mathcal{O}(T^{2}) in prefill and 𝒪 ⁡ ( T ) \mathcal{O}(T) in decode, though with reduced constant factors. This makes ultra-long context still prohibitively expensive. For example, at 10M tokens the indexer takes 98% of the prefill time of traditional sparse attention (Figure  2 ), and a ‘free’ indexer would make sparse attention a further 58 × 58\times faster in prefill and 8.9 × 8.9\times in decode. 
