 Indexer distillation. 
We train on 352M tokens of documents of 16k to 1M tokens from seven domains, for 48k steps, each on 128 queries sampled from one document, with a learning rate of 3 ⋅ 10 − 4 3\cdot 10^{-4} .
This is a fraction of the 2.1B tokens of the dense warm-up of DSA.
In Equation  2 , the target A i A_{i} keeps only the 2,048 keys with the most attention weight per query, renormalised over them, while A ^ i \hat{A}_{i} still spans all of ℛ i \mathcal{R}_{i} , and τ \tau is calibrated per layer before the first step.
Unlike DSA, we do not follow the distillation by training the whole model under sparse attention (sparse training), a step D-LSA also supports but we omit here due to its cost.
We provide further details about the composition and construction of the dataset in Appendix  C . 
 Benchmarks. 
We evaluate accuracy in three categories, each with a representative set of benchmarks.
For knowledge, we use MMLU-Pro  ( Wang et al., 2024 ) (a stratified sample of 2,000 questions) and GPQA-Diamond  ( Rein et al., 2024 ) .
For reasoning, we use AIME25 (four repeats) and MATH500  ( Lightman et al., 2024 ) (100 problems).
For long context, we use LongBench v2  ( Bai et al., 2025 ) (60 questions) and the 13 tasks of RULER  ( Hsieh et al., 2024 ) from 4k to 10M tokens (100 prompts per task up to 1M and 20 beyond).
Evaluating each benchmark in full at ultra-long context is prohibitively expensive, and we therefore use the subsets given in parentheses. 
 PaperTrail. 
To better understand model capabilities in a less synthetic ultra-long context setting, we extend RULER with a set of new tasks called PaperTrail.
The tasks are constructed by embedding a trail of fabricated facts in a large collection of scientific papers.
All papers are recent, almost all published after the release date of the models, such that no model could have seen them in training.
A question can only be answered by finding the facts of its trail and connecting them, as in a literature search.
Samples are built in three domains: exoplanets, long-context machine learning and quantum error correction.
The questions come in three tiers of increasing difficulty, where tier 1 recalls a fact from one paper, tier 2 links facts across two papers and tier 3 traces a trail across three or more.
We provide further details about the construction of PaperTrail in Appendix  D . 
 Model sampling. 
We configure each model’s sampling as recommended by its model card with generation up to 163,840 tokens (Appendix  E ).
 Qwen-3.8-27B reaches 10M tokens with YaRN  ( Peng et al., 2024 ) at factor 40 (42 for PaperTrail at 10M), while GLM-5.3-Flash needs no extension as it has no explicit positional encoding. 
 Hardware and serving. 
We serve all models in SGLang  ( Zheng et al., 2024 ) on H200 and B300 GPUs.
To show that D-LSA can be integrated with the main parallelism strategies, we evaluate it with tensor parallelism (TP8) and with context parallelism in both prefill and decode (CP8). We show more details in Appendix  F.2 .
All accuracy measurements use context parallelism wherever the KV cache of a request outgrows the memory of a single GPU. 
 4.2 Accuracy 
 Figure 5: D-LSA accuracy is on par with full attention and sparse attention baselines on downstream tasks. Shown on knowledge, reasoning and long-context tasks up to 10M tokens. Baseline scores are given with their 95% confidence interval. 
 Figure  5 shows that on knowledge, reasoning and long-context tasks, D-LSA stays close to the accuracy of baseline Full Attention on Qwen-3.8-27B and of baseline sparse attention on GLM-5.3-Flash .
PaperTrail further shows how well the models find and connect facts across large collections of scientific papers. 
 Knowledge and reasoning. 
On knowledge, reasoning and LongBench v2, D-LSA stays within the 95% confidence interval of both baselines, even with reasoning growing to tens of thousands of tokens (Appendix  E ).
We further show that D-LSA does not measurably affect the length of the reasoning. 
 Long context. 
On Qwen-3.8-27B , D-LSA (Flat) stays within 5 points of baseline Full Attention up to 1M tokens and exceeds it at 10M tokens, at 63.9 against 59.9 on RULER and 29.1 against 19.8 on PaperTrail.
It also stays above Block Sparse Attention (Flat) on RULER from 128k tokens on, in a fair comparison, as both indexers are distilled the same way. 
 On GLM-5.3-Flash , D-LSA (Flat) stays within 3 points of baseline sparse attention up to 1M tokens, trails it by up to 8 points at 2M to 5M tokens, and is within 4 points on RULER at 10M tokens.
This comparison favours the baseline, which was trained together with the model, while D-LSA (Flat) is distilled from attention that is already sparse (with no finetuning of the base model itself).
On both models, MultiKey 2, MultiKey 3 and CWE fall the most for every method, the baselines included. We break down RULER by task and PaperTrail by tier in Appendix  E . 
 Design comparison. 
D-LSA (Tree) follows D-LSA (Flat) on Qwen-3.8-27B , but loses accuracy past 1M tokens on GLM-5.3-Flash , at 55.8 against 66.8 on RULER at 10M tokens.
Block Sparse Attention (Tree) falls behind from 512k tokens, to about 63 on RULER at 1M tokens on both models, against 78 and 88 for D-LSA (Tree), and we therefore do not evaluate it beyond 1M tokens.
Large blocks fall earlier (Appendix  E.6 ).
D-LSA (Training-Free), without any training, stays close to D-LSA (Flat) on Qwen-3.8-27B , but trails it on GLM-5.3-Flash . 
 D-LSA follows the accuracy of the baselines, showing that our semantic clustering can preserve accuracy up to 10M tokens. However, we do observe a steep drop in accuracy beyond 512k tokens for Block Sparse Attention (Tree). 
 4.3 Performance 
 Figure 6: D-LSA speedup of one global attention layer. 
Prefill and decode speedup over the baseline attention (log scale), Full Attention for Qwen-3.8-27B and Block Sparse Attention (Flat) for GLM-5.3-Flash , required memory at 10M with one index key per block of four keys for Block Sparse Attention (Flat), and prefill scaling up to 100M (log-log), extrapolated beyond 10M (shaded). 
 Next, we evaluate the performance of D-LSA.
We first measure a single attention layer and then the whole model in production serving. 
 Methodology. 
For one layer, we time the whole attention module, including its projections, the indexer and the attention, on a single GPU.
In prefill, we time the last chunk of 32,768 queries before the given context.
In decode, we time one step of one request with CUDA graph enabled (clustering is amortised across the steps).
For the whole model, we serve each method in SGLang with prefill chunks of 32,768 tokens, and measure the time to first token (TTFT) and the time per output token (TPOT) of one request at a time, the median of three requests of 256 output tokens. 
 Figure  6 shows that the prefill speedup of D-LSA grows with the context.
On GLM-5.3-Flash , it rises from 3.5 × \times at 1M to 22.7 × \times at 10M tokens over baseline sparse attention, and on Qwen-3.8-27B to 72.4 × \times at 10M tokens over baseline Full Attention.
Below 1M tokens, the gain is smaller, at 2.1 × \times at 512k tokens, as the scan of every block is still cheap.
Extrapolated beyond 10M tokens, the prefill of D-LSA (Tree) grows subquadratically and overtakes the flat search, and at 100M tokens it is predicted to be more than 90 × \times faster than baseline sparse attention. 
 Decode follows the same trend, with a speedup up to 4.6 × \times over baseline sparse attention and 42.5 × \times over baseline Full Attention at 10M tokens, with D-LSA (Tree) decoding with the flat search.
On B300, D-LSA reaches 12.5 × \times and 42.9 × \times in prefill and 5.2 × \times and 18.4 × \times in decode at 10M tokens. 
 D-LSA also requires less memory for its indexer.
Baseline sparse attention caches an index key for every block of four keys, while D-LSA caches one centroid in its smaller index space for every bucket of about eight keys, together with the id of each key.
At 10M tokens, the index of D-LSA (Flat) takes 246 MiB per layer against 331 MiB in GLM-5.3-Flash and 642 MiB in QSA.
