HSA  ( Hu et al., 2025 ; Leng et al., 2026 ; Hu et al., 2026a ) and HSA-UltraLong  ( Hu et al., 2026b ) also weight the output of each block by its score, such that the loss trains the scores directly.
All of these methods still scan every block.
PISA  ( Tang et al., 2026 ) replaces this scan with a tree, but evaluates accuracy only at small scale (up to 16k tokens). 
 At the other end, Quest  ( Tang et al., 2024 ) , InfLLM  ( Xiao et al., 2024a ) and MInference  ( Jiang et al., 2024 ) select blocks based on simple summaries of their keys without any training.
HiP  ( Lee et al., 2025a ) searches the blocks with a tree, and its successor InfiniteHiP  ( Lee et al., 2025b ) with three fixed stages.
BLASST  ( Yuan et al., 2026 ) instead skips blocks directly inside the attention kernel, without a dedicated indexer.
Most of these methods summarise blocks of consecutive keys, which may hide a single relevant key  ( Xiao et al., 2024a ; Hu et al., 2026a ) . 
 Closest to LSA are methods that organise the keys by semantic content rather than by position.
A few early works train at short context, with Reformer  ( Kitaev et al., 2020 ) hashing keys into buckets, Routing Transformer  ( Roy et al., 2021 ) learning k-means centroids, and Treeformer  ( Madaan et al., 2023 ) routing each query down a learned tree.
Most other works here only operate at inference time: ClusterKV  ( Liu et al., 2025b ) and RetroInfer  ( Chen et al., 2026 ) cluster the keys during prefill and score every centroid at decode (flat search), Squeezed Attention  ( Hooper et al., 2025b ) , Multipole Attention  ( Hooper et al., 2025a ) and LycheeCluster  ( Li et al., 2026 ) search two levels (fixed search), and IceCache  ( Mao et al., 2026b ) descends a tree of keys (tree search), while RetrievalAttention  ( Liu et al., 2025a ) links the keys in a nearest-neighbour graph.
All of these inference methods are training-free, only Squeezed Attention speeds up prefill, and only partially, and none are evaluated at ultra-long context (above 1M tokens). 
 Figure 3: D-LSA design. Left: D-LSA (Flat). Indexer keys: 1 projection and L2 norm, 2 pooling (4:1), 3 bottom-up agglomerative clustering (Ward’s method), 4 bucket cache (centroids and index table). Indexer queries: A sum over query heads, projection and L2 norm, B scoring the cached centroids and selecting the top-p buckets per query, C expanding the buckets into indices (sink + top-k long-range + window), D attention over those indices (one index list per query shared by all query heads). Right: D-LSA (Tree) groups the buckets into a hierarchy of clusters, a query descends using beam search (close in the latent space but scattered through the KV cache). 
 3 Latent Sparse Attention (LSA) 
 LSA is a sparse attention layer for ultra-long context.
It clusters keys by content, in a space that is learned: through index projections in D-LSA, and through the attention’s own keys in N-LSA.
LSA can replace the attention of a pre-trained layer by distillation (D-LSA) or be trained natively with the model (N-LSA). 
 In both settings, the indices for the attention are selected from the keys and queries by the same process.
We first describe this process, numbered as in Figure  3 , which consists of semantic clustering of the index keys k j ( idx ) k_{j}^{(\mathrm{idx})} , and the search with the index queries q i ( idx ) q_{i}^{(\mathrm{idx})} so that each query selects its keys.
We then describe the specifics of D-LSA and N-LSA. 
 3.1 Semantic Clustering 
 1 
 Key normalisation. 
Each index key k j ( idx ) k_{j}^{(\mathrm{idx})} is first L2 normalised: k j ( idx ) ← k j ( idx ) ∥ k j ( idx ) ∥ k_{j}^{(\mathrm{idx})}\leftarrow\frac{k_{j}^{(\mathrm{idx})}}{\lVert k_{j}^{(\mathrm{idx})}\rVert} 
 2 
 Pooling. 
Consecutive keys are then compressed, as in CSA  ( DeepSeek-AI, 2026a ) , QSA  ( Qwen Team, 2026a ) and GLM-5.3-Flash SA  ( Z.ai, 2026 ) , to improve computational efficiency. Here, every n pool n_{\mathrm{pool}} consecutive index keys are summed into one pool key k pool ( idx ) k_{\mathrm{pool}}^{(\mathrm{idx})} , so that clustering runs over T / n pool T/n_{\mathrm{pool}} pool keys rather than T T keys. 
 3 
 Clustering. 
Keys are clustered in cluster blocks of n cluster n_{\mathrm{cluster}} consecutive keys.
Once a cluster block contains all its keys, its pool keys are clustered bottom up with Ward’s method  ( Ward, 1963 ) into m m buckets (Algorithm  1 in Appendix  B ).
Each pool key starts as a bucket of its own, and each bucket keeps the sum of its keys, whose normalisation is its centroid c b c_{b} .
In every round, all pairs of buckets that are each other’s cheapest merge are merged in parallel.
The cost of a merge is how much it increases the keys’ total squared distance from their centroid (Ward’s criterion).
Merging stops once there are m = n cluster / n ¯ b m=n_{\mathrm{cluster}}/\bar{n}_{b} buckets, where n ¯ b \bar{n}_{b} is the average number of keys per bucket, such that every cluster block holds the same number of buckets.
A merge is skipped if the merged bucket would hold more than n b max n_{b}^{\mathrm{max}} keys. 
 4 
 Bucket cache. 
The bucket cache B cache ( idx ) B_{\mathrm{cache}}^{(\mathrm{idx})} stores the centroid c b c_{b} of every bucket and an index table of the positions of its keys. This is so later selected buckets can ‘expand’ into their key indices. With one centroid per n ¯ b \bar{n}_{b} keys, it corresponds to a small fraction of the KV cache. 
 Once a cluster block is clustered, its buckets never change. In the Tree variant, this clustering process is repeated to group the buckets into multiple levels of a hierarchy. 
 3.2 Semantic Search 
 We now describe how the top- k k key indices are extracted for each query, and then submitted to attention. We first describe the process for Flat , in which there is only one level of buckets, and then discuss Tree . 
 A 
 Query normalisation. 
Each index query q i ( idx ) q_{i}^{(\mathrm{idx})} is first L2 normalised: q i ( idx ) ← q i ( idx ) ∥ q i ( idx ) ∥ q_{i}^{(\mathrm{idx})}\leftarrow\frac{q_{i}^{(\mathrm{idx})}}{\lVert q_{i}^{(\mathrm{idx})}\rVert} 
 B 
 Scoring. 
The index query scores the unit-norm centroid of every bucket in the cluster blocks before its own via s i , b = c b ⋅ q i ( idx ) s_{i,b}=c_{b}\cdot q_{i}^{(\mathrm{idx})} , which compares buckets of any size on one scale, and selects the top- p p buckets.
Its own cluster block is not clustered yet, and its keys are covered by the window instead. 
 C 
 Expansion. 
The selected p p buckets are expanded into their keys, best first, until k k ‘long-range’ keys are selected.
As every bucket holds at least n pool n_{\mathrm{pool}} keys, p p is chosen such that p ⋅ n pool ≥ k p\cdot n_{\mathrm{pool}}\geq k , and so expansion always fills k k keys. 
 Besides the k k long-range keys, every query attends to n sink n_{\mathrm{sink}} sink keys  ( Xiao et al., 2024b ) and the n w n_{w} most recent keys (window).
A key of a selected bucket that falls in the sink or the window is already attended to, and the expansion moves on to the next key instead.
We set the window to one cluster block, n w = n cluster n_{w}=n_{\mathrm{cluster}} , so that the window covers every key not yet clustered.
This gives each query i i a set J i J_{i} of at most n sink + k + n w n_{\mathrm{sink}}+k+n_{w} key indices. 
 D 
 Attention. 
Attention is then computed only over the keys indexed by J i J_{i} . A single J i J_{i} is shared across all query heads. 
 Tree search arranges the buckets into a hierarchy (Figure  3 , right).
Level 0 holds the buckets, and each higher level ℓ ≥ 1 \ell\geq 1 holds buckets we term ‘node buckets’, each made of n b , ℓ n_{b,\ell} buckets of the level below, with a centroid over all the keys it contains.
As at level 0, buckets are merged one cluster block at a time.
Buckets that are not merged yet are defined as open.
Once a level holds n cluster , ℓ n_{\mathrm{cluster},\ell} open buckets, they are merged into node buckets as in Algorithm  1 . 
 Beam search is used to select the buckets through the hierarchy (Algorithm  2 in Appendix  B ).
On each level, the query is scored against the centroids of the candidate node buckets, and the best p ℓ p_{\ell} are kept.
The candidates on the level below are the children of the kept node buckets and the open buckets of that level.
At level 0, the top- p p buckets are selected, as in the flat search.
With a constant beam per level, a query scores 𝒪 ⁡ ( log ⁡ T ) \mathcal{O}(\log T) centroids, which makes the prefill of the indexer subquadratic, 𝒪 ⁡ ( T ​ log ⁡ T ) \mathcal{O}(T\log T) , where the flat search is quadratic. 
 3.3 D-LSA 
 D-LSA (Figure  3 ) follows the process outlined above, but since it distils from a pre-trained model which already has key and query projections ( k j ( attn ) k_{j}^{(\mathrm{attn})} and q i ( attn ) q_{i}^{(\mathrm{attn})} ), we additionally learn separate key and query projections for LSA. In particular, each key k j ( attn ) k_{j}^{(\mathrm{attn})} and query q i ( attn ) q_{i}^{(\mathrm{attn})} is projected to a smaller dimension d ( idx ) d^{(\mathrm{idx})} to keep the scoring, clustering, and bucket cache cheap: 
 k j ( idx ) \displaystyle k_{j}^{(\mathrm{idx})} 
 = k j ( attn ) ​ W K ( idx ) \displaystyle=k_{j}^{(\mathrm{attn})}W_{K}^{(\mathrm{idx})} 
 (1) 
 q i ( idx ) \displaystyle q_{i}^{(\mathrm{idx})} 
 = ∑ h q i , h ( attn ) ​ W Q ( idx ) \displaystyle=\sum_{h}q_{i,h}^{(\mathrm{attn})}W_{Q}^{(\mathrm{idx})} 
 The query heads are summed such that one index query serves every head, which yields the single index list J i J_{i} per query.
On Qwen-3.8-27B , whose keys and queries carry RoPE  ( Su et al., 2024 ) on a quarter of their dimensions, the projections read only the RoPE-free part.
These keys and queries then go into the normalisation process as before.
Trainable projections can also be replaced by the leading d ( idx ) d^{(\mathrm{idx})} principal directions of the keys in the layer, which project both keys and queries into one index space with no training.
We call this variant D-LSA (Training-Free). 
 The base model’s attention is otherwise unchanged: its own queries attend only to the keys and values indexed by J i J_{i} . 
 Distillation. 
D-LSA adds LSA to a pre-trained model by a short distillation that trains only W Q , K ( idx ) W_{Q,K}^{(\mathrm{idx})} and a temperature τ \tau per layer, with the base model frozen.
This mirrors the dense warm-up of DSA  ( DeepSeek-AI, 2025 ) , which also trains only the indexer against the head-aggregated attention of the frozen model.
The distillation scores every key directly, without clustering.
For a query i i of a layer, let ℛ i \mathcal{R}_{i} be the keys it can select (all preceding keys outside the sink and the window).
The target A i A_{i} is the attention of that frozen layer over ℛ i \mathcal{R}_{i} , the softmax of each head’s logits over ℛ i \mathcal{R}_{i} averaged over its n h n_{h} heads of dimension d h d_{h} , and A ^ i \hat{A}_{i} is the softmax of the D-LSA scores over the same keys.
The distillation minimises the forward KL divergence between the two: 
 ℒ i \displaystyle\mathcal{L}_{i} 
 = KL ( A i ∥ A ^ i ) \displaystyle=\mathrm{KL}\big(A_{i}\,\|\,\hat{A}_{i}\big) 
 (2) 
 A i , j \displaystyle A_{i,j} 
 = 1 n h ​ ∑ h softmax j ∈ ℛ i ​ ( q i , h ( attn ) ⋅ k j ( attn ) / d h ) \displaystyle=\tfrac{1}{n_{h}}\textstyle\sum_{h}\mathrm{softmax}_{j\in\mathcal{R}_{i}}\big(q_{i,h}^{(\mathrm{attn})}\cdot k_{j}^{(\mathrm{attn})}/\sqrt{d_{h}}\big) 
 A ^ i , j \displaystyle\hat{A}_{i,j} 
 = softmax j ∈ ℛ i ​ ( q i ( idx ) ⋅ k j ( idx ) / τ ) \displaystyle=\mathrm{softmax}_{j\in\mathcal{R}_{i}}\big(q_{i}^{(\mathrm{idx})}\cdot k_{j}^{(\mathrm{idx})}/\tau\big) 
 The temperature τ \tau sharpens the softmax of the D-LSA scores to match the peaked attention of the model, and is used only in this loss.
Both projections are initialised with these principal directions. 
 Kernels. 
We write GPU kernels for the clustering, scoring, selection and expansion of the indexer, in CuTe DSL for H200 and B300, with a Triton version of each, which runs on any GPU.
For the attention, we reuse existing attention kernels, such as FlashAttention-3  ( Shah et al., 2024 ) , which read the index list J i J_{i} as a page table. 
 Parallelism. 
We integrate LSA into SGLang with support for tensor, expert, and pipeline parallelism, and for context parallelism in both prefill and decode.
Context parallelism splits the KV cache across ranks by token, but LSA replicates its bucket cache on every rank.
Every rank thus builds the same index and selects the same index list. The ranks only exchange the index keys of the newest tokens.
This design choice trades memory for communication.
